Description: Fix GHSA-q4gf-8mx6-v5v3 (CVE-2026-23869) - bound RSC deserialization work to prevent CPU DoS
 Next.js's vendored react-server-dom-webpack Flight decoder
 (decodeReply/initializeModelChunk) parsed the raw JSON model string of an
 untrusted request body via JSON.parse without any bound on structural
 (array/object) nesting depth, allowing an attacker to send a deeply nested
 payload that exhausts CPU during parsing/reviver execution and causes a
 denial of service. This patch adds an O(n) string-literal-aware scan
 (enforceModelStringLimits in the unminified dev bundles, inlined as
 __rscSecGuard in the minified production bundles) that walks chunk.value
 before JSON.parse is invoked, tracking bracket/brace nesting depth while
 ignoring braces/brackets inside string literals (respecting backslash
 escapes), and throws once depth exceeds a configurable limit (default
 1000, read from response._arraySizeLimit). When an explicit limit is
 configured it also accumulates a running total of structural nodes across
 chunks on response._arrayNodeCount and rejects once the total exceeds
 limit*limit + limit. createResponse/decodeReply gain a new trailing
 options parameter (backward compatible via an arguments.length check) so
 callers can opt into an explicit arraySizeLimit.
